    summary:
      'Any surface that is not already a topological disk must be cut before it can be flattened to a disk-like texture domain — this includes every closed surface (even the genus-0 sphere, χ = 2), every surface with handles (g ≥ 1), and any surface with zero or more than one boundary loop. A surface unwraps without cuts iff it is homeomorphic to a disk (simply connected, exactly one boundary loop, χ = 1). Reducing a connected orientable genus-g surface with b boundary loops to a single disk needs 2g + max(b−1, 0) cuts when b ≥ 1, and max(2g, 1) cuts when b = 0 — that is, 2g cuts for a closed genus-g surface with g ≥ 1 (e.g. 2 for the torus, matching the two homology generators of the cut-graphs example), while the genus-0 sphere is the special closed case needing exactly 1 cut. Nonorientable surfaces (Möbius band, Klein bottle, RP²) are governed instead by the crosscap number k (χ = 2 − k − b) and follow a crosscap-based cut count rather than this 2g formula.',
