    "tier": "foundations",
    "symbol": "{ }",
    "idea": "A hash map/set trades space for time: by hashing keys into buckets you turn \"does this value exist?\" and \"how many times have I seen this?\" from an O(n) scan into expected O(1) lookup/insert. The core trick is to remember what you've already seen so that, as you sweep an array once, the answer for the current element is already available in the table. This converts naive O(n^2) double-loops (compare every pair) into a single O(n) pass: for each element, you query the map for its complement, its partner, its frequency, or its group key. It works because hashing distributes keys near-uniformly across buckets, making the amortized cost of each operation constant regardless of the value's magnitude or range.",
    "whenToUse": "Reach for hashing when the problem asks about existence, membership, duplicates, \"have I seen X before\", complement/pair search (a + b = target), frequency/counts/mode, grouping items by a canonical signature (anagrams, sorted key), set operations (intersection/union/difference), or deduplication. Strong signals: a brute-force solution is a nested loop comparing pairs; the question says \"in O(n)\" or \"single pass\"; you need to map a derived key (sorted string, character count tuple, normalized value) to a list of originals; or you must detect \"first/only unique\" or \"k-th most frequent\". If order or proximity also matters, you usually combine the map with sliding-window or two-pointers.",
    "pattern": "seen = {}                      # value -> index, or set, or count\nfor i, x in enumerate(arr):\n    # complement search:\n    if (target - x) in seen:\n        return [seen[target - x], i]\n    seen[x] = i\n# frequency counting:\ncnt = Counter(arr)             # value -> occurrences\n# grouping by canonical key:\ngroups = defaultdict(list)\nfor s in strs:\n    key = tuple(sorted(s))     # or a 26-length count tuple\n    groups[key].append(s)\n# dedup / membership:\nunique = set(arr); exists = x in unique",
