    "id": "heap-pq",
    "title": "Heaps / priority queues",
    "tier": "structures",
    "symbol": "PQ",
    "idea": "A binary heap is a complete binary tree (stored as a flat array) where every parent dominates its children by some key, so the extremum sits at the root and is readable in O(1). Insert and extract restore the heap-order invariant by sifting an element up or down one level at a time along the tree's height, which is log n. The win is that you never pay to fully sort: when you only need the running min/max, the next item to process, or the k best, a heap gives you cheap incremental access to the current extremum while spending only log n per update — strictly less than re-sorting and strictly more flexible than a single pass. This is exactly the structure that powers greedy \"always take the best available option next\" algorithms and lazy ordered frontiers.",
    "whenToUse": "Reach for a heap when you need the k largest/smallest or the kth element of a stream without full sorting; when you repeatedly need \"the current min/max\" of a changing multiset (running median, scheduling the earliest-finishing job, merging sorted runs); when a greedy algorithm must always pick the best-scoring candidate next (Dijkstra/Prim frontiers, task scheduling by deadline/profit, connecting ropes/merging files by smallest cost); or when you process events in priority order and new events get pushed mid-flight. Signal words: \"top k\", \"kth largest\", \"k closest/smallest\", \"merge k sorted\", \"minimum cost to combine\", \"schedule with cooldown/deadline\", \"shortest path with weights\". If you need the full sorted order or arbitrary-position deletes, prefer sorting or a balanced BST instead.",
    "pattern": "Core ops on an array-backed binary heap: parent(i)=(i-1)/2, children 2i+1, 2i+2; push = append then sift-up; pop = swap root with last, shrink, sift-down. Most languages give you one: Python heapq (min-heap; push negatives or tuples for max/custom keys), Java PriorityQueue(Comparator), C++ priority_queue (max-heap by default). Templates: (1) Top-k largest with a size-k MIN-heap: push each element, if size>k pop the smallest; the heap holds the k largest, root is the kth largest. O(n log k). (2) k-way merge: push the head (value, listIndex, nodeIndex) of each of k lists; repeatedly pop the min, output it, push its successor. (3) Two-heap median: a max-heap for the lower half and a min-heap for the upper half, rebalanced so sizes differ by <=1; median is a root or average of two roots. (4) Greedy/Dijkstra frontier: push (priority, node); pop smallest, skip if stale (dist already finalized), else relax neighbors and push updated tentative costs (lazy deletion). (5) Heapify an array in O(n) when you have all elements up front, then pop k times for partial sort.",
    "complexity": "Build heap (heapify) O(n); push/pop O(log n); peek O(1). Top-k via size-k heap O(n log k) time, O(k) space. k-way merge of N total elements across k lists: O(N log k) time, O(k) space for the heap. Two-heap running median: O(log n) per insertion, O(1) per query. Dijkstra with binary heap: O((V+E) log V) with lazy deletion (heap may hold up to O(E) stale entries → O(E) space). Heapsort overall O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space. Note search for an arbitrary value or decrease-key by value is O(n) in a binary heap unless you maintain an index map.",
    "pitfalls": "Min-heap vs max-heap confusion: Python heapq and many libs are MIN-heaps — negate keys or use a max-heap explicitly for \"largest\". For top-k LARGEST use a MIN-heap of size k (counterintuitive); for k SMALLEST use a MAX-heap of size k. Tuple comparison traps: when pushing (priority, item), a tie on priority makes the heap compare the items themselves, which throws if items aren't comparable (e.g. ListNode, dict) — add a unique tiebreaker counter as the middle element. Binary heaps support decrease-key only in O(n) without an index map; standard interview Dijkstra uses lazy deletion (push duplicates, skip stale pops) instead. Forgetting to skip stale entries in Dijkstra/Prim corrupts results or wastes work. Heapify is O(n), but pushing n items one by one is O(n log n) — use the bulk constructor when you have all data. Don't use a heap when a simpler structure wins: full sort beats a heap if you need every element ordered; Quickselect gives O(n) average for a single kth element vs O(n log k); bucket/counting sort beats a heap for top-k by frequency in O(n). The root is the kth extremum only after the size-k invariant is maintained throughout.",
