    "symbol": "DSU",
    "idea": "A disjoint-set forest represents each connected component as a tree whose root is the component's canonical id; two elements are connected iff find() walks them to the same root. The magic is in two optimizations: union by rank/size always hangs the shorter tree under the taller one (keeping trees bushy), and path compression flattens the find() path by re-pointing every node directly to the root as you climb. Together they make a tree so shallow that any sequence of m operations costs only O(m alpha(n)) total, where alpha is the inverse-Ackermann function and is at most ~4 for any conceivable n. It works because connectivity is an equivalence relation (reflexive, symmetric, transitive), and a forest of pointers-to-representative is the cheapest data structure that maintains equivalence classes under incremental merges.",
    "whenToUse": "Reach for DSU when the problem is about grouping/merging that only ever ADDS connections (no edge deletions) and you need to answer \"are these two in the same group?\" or \"how many groups / how big is this group?\". Signals: dynamic connectivity, counting connected components as edges arrive, detecting a cycle in an UNDIRECTED graph (an edge whose endpoints already share a root closes a cycle), equivalence classes (accounts/emails to merge, equations like a/b=k, string equality/inequality constraints), grid flood-grouping when you also want incremental updates (\"number of islands II\"), and Kruskal's MST where you greedily add the cheapest edge unless it would form a cycle. If the problem needs deletions/rollback, reach for offline tricks (process in reverse) or a link-cut tree instead. If you only need a one-shot component count on a static graph, plain BFS/DFS is simpler.",
    "pattern": "parent = list(range(n)); rank = [0]*n; count = n  # count = #components\n\ndef find(x):                       # iterative path compression\n    root = x\n    while parent[root] != root: root = parent[root]\n    while parent[x] != root:       # second pass flattens the path\n        parent[x], x = root, parent[x]\n    return root\n\ndef union(a, b):                   # union by rank; returns False if already joined\n    ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n    if ra == rb: return False\n    if rank[ra] < rank[rb]: ra, rb = rb, ra\n    parent[rb] = ra\n    if rank[ra] == rank[rb]: rank[ra] += 1\n    count -= 1\n    return True\n\n# Kruskal's MST: sort edges by weight, union endpoints, take edge iff union() True,\n#   stop after n-1 edges. Total weight is the sum of accepted edge weights.\n# Weighted DSU (a/b = k): store rank weight[x] = ratio to parent; combine on find/union.",
    "complexity": "Time: each find/union is O(alpha(n)) amortized (effectively O(1)); m operations cost O(m alpha(n)). With only path compression OR only union-by-rank, it is O(log n) amortized; with neither it degrades to O(n) per op. Kruskal is O(E log E) dominated by the edge sort, plus O(E alpha(V)) for the DSU work. Space: O(n) for the parent (and rank/size) arrays.",
    "pitfalls": "Forgetting BOTH optimizations: without union-by-rank/size and path compression the forest can become a linked list and find() goes O(n). Recursive find() can blow the stack on adversarial chains; prefer the iterative two-pass version. Comparing parent[a]==parent[b] instead of find(a)==find(b) (immediate parents differ from roots). Decrementing the component count even when the two elements were already in the same set (only decrement when union actually merges). Mixing union by rank with union by size — pick one and be consistent; rank is an upper bound on height, not the exact size. Path compression invalidates rank as a true height, but that is fine because rank stays a valid upper bound. For weighted DSU (ratios, parity) you must update the weight/offset during path compression, not just the parent. Off-by-one in id mapping when nodes are strings, emails, or 2D grid cells (use r*cols+c). DSU cannot handle edge deletion or directed-graph cycle detection.",
