    "id": "shortest-paths",
    "title": "Shortest paths",
    "tier": "graphs",
    "symbol": "δ(u,v)",
    "idea": "All three classic algorithms are built on one operation: edge relaxation. A tentative distance dist[v] is an upper bound on the true shortest distance; relaxing edge (u,v,w) does dist[v] = min(dist[v], dist[u]+w). The correctness is an induction/DP argument: once dist[u] is provably final, any edge out of u can correctly tighten its neighbors. Dijkstra orders relaxations greedily by smallest tentative distance (valid only with nonnegative weights, because settled vertices can never be improved later); Bellman-Ford brute-forces V-1 full passes of relaxation (after k passes every shortest path of k edges is correct), which tolerates negative edges and detects negative cycles via a V-th pass that still relaxes; Floyd-Warshall is a DP over an allowed-intermediate-vertex set k, dist[i][j] = min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k]+dist[k][j]). The LP dual of shortest path assigns each vertex a potential/price h(v); the optimality (feasibility) condition h(v) <= h(u)+w(u,v) is exactly \"no edge can be relaxed,\" and Johnson's algorithm uses such potentials to reweight negative edges to nonnegative so Dijkstra applies.",
    "whenToUse": "You need a minimum-cost path in a weighted graph (cost = distance, time, money, risk, max-probability via logs, or min-of-max bottleneck via a relaxation tweak). Signals: \"minimum/cheapest/fastest path\", \"minimum cost to reach\", weighted grid movement, \"k stops\"/\"at most k edges\" (Bellman-Ford style bounded relaxation), \"shortest path with state\" (split nodes by extra dimension then Dijkstra). Choose by weights: nonnegative single-source -> Dijkstra (heap); possible negative edges or \"exactly/at most k edges\" -> Bellman-Ford; need all-pairs and V is small (<=400-500) -> Floyd-Warshall. If all weights equal, plain BFS beats all of these.",
    "pattern": "Dijkstra (heap):\n  dist=[inf]*n; dist[src]=0; pq=[(0,src)]\n  while pq:\n    d,u=heappop(pq)\n    if d>dist[u]: continue            # stale entry, skip\n    for v,w in adj[u]:\n      if d+w < dist[v]:\n        dist[v]=d+w; heappush(pq,(dist[v],v))\n\nBellman-Ford:\n  dist=[inf]*n; dist[src]=0\n  for i in range(n-1):                 # n-1 passes\n    for u,v,w in edges:\n      if dist[u]+w < dist[v]: dist[v]=dist[u]+w\n  for u,v,w in edges:                  # extra pass detects neg cycle\n    if dist[u]+w < dist[v]: return \"negative cycle\"\n  # bounded variant (\"<=k edges\"): loop k+1 times over a SNAPSHOT of dist\n\nFloyd-Warshall:\n  for k in range(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n      for j in range(n):\n        if dist[i][k]+dist[k][j] < dist[i][j]:\n          dist[i][j]=dist[i][k]+dist[k][j]\n  # dist[i][i]<0 after the run => i lies on a negative cycle",
    "complexity": "Dijkstra with binary heap: O((V+E) log V) time, O(V+E) space (O(E log V) dominant on dense graphs; Fibonacci heap gives O(E + V log V) in theory). Bellman-Ford: O(V*E) time, O(V) space; bounded-k variant O(k*E). Floyd-Warshall: O(V^3) time, O(V^2) space. Johnson's (all-pairs with negatives): O(V*E + V*E log V) = one Bellman-Ford plus V Dijkstras.",
    "pitfalls": "Dijkstra is wrong with any negative edge — do not use it there. Use lazy deletion: push duplicates and skip stale pops (if d>dist[u]) rather than a decrease-key. Watch integer overflow: guard dist[u]+w only when dist[u]!=inf (in Bellman-Ford/Floyd-Warshall an inf+w can falsely relax). Bellman-Ford needs exactly V-1 passes for correctness; for the \"at most k edges\" variant you MUST relax against a snapshot/previous-layer copy of dist so one pass can't chain through edges added in the same pass. Floyd-Warshall's loop order is k-outermost — swapping loops breaks it. For max-probability use -log(p) and Dijkstra (or relax with multiplication and a max-heap); for bottleneck/min-max-edge change the relaxation to max(dist[u], w). Forgetting to skip stale heap entries still gives the right answer but degrades performance. Negative-cycle detection: Bellman-Ford = relaxation still possible on pass V; Floyd-Warshall = some dist[i][i]<0.",
