    "id": "bit-manipulation",
    "title": "Bit manipulation",
    "tier": "paradigms",
    "symbol": "b&1",
    "idea": "An integer is just a fixed-length vector of bits, and the CPU gives you O(1) parallel operations over all of them at once: AND/OR/XOR/NOT, shifts, and lowbit tricks. The key structural fact is that XOR turns bit-vectors into the abelian group (Z/2)^n — every element is its own inverse (x^x=0) and 0 is the identity, so XOR-ing a value an even number of times cancels it. That cancellation is the engine behind \"find the unique/missing element\" problems and behind toggling state cheaply. Treating a subset of n items as the n bits of an integer lets you enumerate, intersect, and union subsets with raw machine ops, which is what makes bitmask DP feasible.",
    "whenToUse": "Reach for bits when: (1) you need to track a SET of up to ~20-22 small items as state and want O(1) membership/union/intersection — that's bitmask DP; (2) elements pair up or cancel and you want the lonely one (XOR cancellation); (3) you must enumerate all subsets, or all subsets of a subset, efficiently; (4) constraints scream \"n ≤ 20\", \"n ≤ 1e7 with O(1) extra space\", or \"without using +/-\"; (5) you're counting set bits, finding the lowest/highest set bit, or doing arithmetic via shifts; (6) a problem decomposes per-bit independently (answer each of the 32 bit-positions separately).",
    "pattern": "Core idioms:\n  get  bit i:  (x >> i) & 1\n  set  bit i:  x | (1 << i)\n  clear bit i: x & ~(1 << i)\n  flip bit i:  x ^ (1 << i)\n  lowbit (lowest set bit):  x & (-x)\n  remove lowbit (Brian Kernighan): x & (x - 1)   // loop to count bits\n  is power of two: x>0 && (x & (x-1))==0\n  full mask of n items: (1 << n) - 1\n\nXOR cancellation (single number): ans=0; for v in a: ans^=v; return ans.\n\nEnumerate all subsets: for m in 0..(1<<n): ...\nEnumerate submasks of mask: for(s=mask; s; s=(s-1)&mask){...}  // plus empty set\n\nBitmask DP skeleton (e.g. assignment / TSP / cover):\n  dp[mask] = best using the chosen set 'mask'\n  for mask in 0..(1<<n):\n    for j where bit j NOT in mask:\n      relax dp[mask | (1<<j)] from dp[mask] + cost(...)\n  Iterate cleared bits via (((1<<n)-1) ^ mask).",
    "complexity": "Per-op AND/OR/XOR/shift/lowbit: O(1) on machine words (O(W/64) for big integers). Brian Kernighan popcount: O(set bits). Enumerate all subsets of n items: O(2^n). Enumerate all submasks of all masks: O(3^n) total (each element is in/out/not-in-parent). Bitmask DP: states 2^n times transition work — typically O(2^n · n) (assignment, set cover) or O(2^n · n^2) (TSP / Hamiltonian path). Space O(2^n) for the dp table, so practical only for n ≲ 20-22.",
    "pitfalls": "- 1 << 31 / 1 << k overflows signed 32-bit int in C++/Java; use 1L << k for 64-bit masks. In Python ints are unbounded (no overflow, but also no fixed width — emulate 32-bit with & 0xFFFFFFFF and reinterpret sign).\n- Operator precedence: `x & 1 == 0` parses as `x & (1==0)`. Parenthesize: `(x & 1) == 0`.\n- Right shift of negatives: arithmetic vs logical (>> vs >>> in Java) differs — sign bit propagation bugs.\n- lowbit needs two's-complement; `-x` of INT_MIN is itself (UB in C++ signed overflow) — use unsigned.\n- Forgetting the empty submask: `(s-1)&mask` loop stops before s=0, so handle empty set separately if needed.\n- Bitmask DP iteration order: process masks in increasing numeric order so dp[mask] is final before it relaxes supersets (a superset is always numerically larger).\n- Confusing \"subset of items\" mask with \"value of element\"; off-by-one between bit index and 1-based item label.\n- Counting: n>20-ish makes 2^n infeasible — bitmask DP is NOT a general DP; check constraints first.",
