    "id": "strings",
    "title": "String matching (KMP / Z / rolling hash)",
    "tier": "paradigms",
    "symbol": "π/Z/h",
    "idea": "All three techniques replace the naive O(nm) \"compare-the-pattern-everywhere\" scan with linear-time matching by precomputing self-overlap structure. KMP builds a failure (prefix) function π where π[i] = length of the longest proper prefix of the pattern that is also a suffix of pattern[0..i]; on a mismatch you slide the pattern by following π instead of restarting, so the text pointer never moves backward. The Z-algorithm computes, for each position i, the length of the longest substring starting at i that matches a prefix of the whole string, reusing a maintained [l,r] window so each character is examined O(1) amortized times. Rolling hash maps a string window to a number under a polynomial hash h = Σ s[i]·B^(len−1−i) (mod a large prime); sliding the window updates the hash in O(1) by subtracting the leading term, multiplying by the base, and adding the new char — turning substring equality into integer equality, with the prime modulus making collisions provably rare. The reason all work: matching is fundamentally about reusing information already gathered about the string's own periodic/overlap structure rather than rediscovering it.",
    "whenToUse": "Reach for these when you must find/count occurrences of a pattern in text, detect periodicity or borders (prefixes that are also suffixes), test if a string is a rotation/repetition of another, or compare many substrings for equality fast. Concrete signals: \"find the index of the first occurrence\", \"shortest palindrome by prepending\", \"longest happy/repeated prefix-suffix\", \"longest duplicate substring\", \"does string A appear in B\", \"count distinct substrings\", or any time naive O(nm) substring comparison would TLE on inputs up to 10^5–10^6. Prefer rolling hash when you need to compare arbitrary substrings or binary-search on a length (Longest Duplicate Substring); prefer KMP/Z when you need exact borders, periods, or guaranteed worst-case correctness without collision risk.",
    "pattern": "KMP prefix function (build π for pattern p):\n  pi=[0]*m; k=0\n  for i in 1..m-1:\n    while k>0 and p[i]!=p[k]: k=pi[k-1]\n    if p[i]==p[k]: k+=1\n    pi[i]=k\n  Search text t: run the same loop over t against p, on full match (k==m) record i-m+1 and set k=pi[k-1].\n\nZ-algorithm (Z[i]=lcp(s, s[i:])):\n  Z=[0]*n; l=r=0\n  for i in 1..n-1:\n    if i<r: Z[i]=min(r-i, Z[i-l])\n    while i+Z[i]<n and s[Z[i]]==s[i+Z[i]]: Z[i]+=1\n    if i+Z[i]>r: l,i wait -> l=i; r=i+Z[i]\n  Match by running Z on  pattern + '#' + text; Z[i]==m flags an occurrence.\n\nRolling (polynomial) hash, mod prime M, base B (random in [256, M)):\n  pow[0]=1; pow[k]=pow[k-1]*B % M\n  pre[0]=0; pre[i+1]=(pre[i]*B + s[i]) % M\n  hash(l,r) = (pre[r+1] - pre[l]*pow[r-l+1]) % M   # substring s[l..r]\n  Use TWO independent (M,B) pairs (double hashing) to crush collision probability; verify a candidate by direct char compare when correctness is critical.",
    "complexity": "KMP and Z: O(n+m) time, O(m) (KMP) or O(n+m) (Z) extra space — n=text, m=pattern; the text pointer is monotone and each amortized step is O(1). Rolling-hash single match: O(n+m) expected; with binary search on length (e.g. longest duplicate substring) O((n+m) log n) expected. Single hash has collision probability ~ (#comparisons)/M per pair; with a 64-bit prime or two 32-bit primes it is negligible. Space O(n) for prefix-hash and power tables.",
    "pitfalls": "KMP: π[i] is the length (so the next index to compare is π[i]); fall back with k=pi[k-1], not k-=1. Off-by-one when recording a match: index is i-m+1. Z-algorithm: the separator '#' between pattern and text must not occur in either string; clamp Z[i]=min(r-i, Z[i-l]) before extending. Rolling hash: NEVER use the default Java hashCode/small modulus — adversarial/anti-hash tests cause collisions; use a large random prime and a random base, and prefer double hashing. Watch C/C++ overflow: multiply in 64-bit (or use __int128) before taking mod; in Python ints are arbitrary precision but still mod to keep numbers small. Handle the modular subtraction in hash(l,r) by adding M before the final mod to avoid negatives. Empty pattern, pattern longer than text, and a pattern equal to the whole string are common edge cases. Don't confuse \"longest prefix that is also suffix\" (border, what π gives) with \"longest palindromic prefix\" (different problem).",
