    "intuition": "Picture the rotations of 3D space SO(3) as a solid ball of radius pi: each point is \"rotate by that angle about that axis,\" but antipodal points on the boundary are the same rotation, which glues the ball into a space (RP^3) where some loops cannot be shrunk to a point — that is the famous belt/plate trick. Now lift to the 3-sphere S^3 sitting in quaternion space: it is the same rotations but each one is named twice (by q and by -q), and S^3 has no un-shrinkable loops, so the tangle untwists. The covering map S^3 -> RP^3 wrapping twice IS the statement SU(2) = Spin(3) double-covers SO(3). The half-angle in q = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u is the smoking gun: rotors act by a two-sided sandwich, so the angle gets used twice and each factor only needs half. Lie algebra view: near the identity all these groups look like a flat vector space of \"infinitesimal rotations\" (bivectors / anti-Hermitian matrices / pure imaginary quaternions), and the exponential map wraps that flat space around the curved group.",
