    "id": "gt-axioms",
    "title": "Groups & axioms",
    "tier": "foundations",
    "symbol": "G",
    "idea": "A group is the minimal algebraic structure capturing \"reversible composition\": a set G with one binary operation that is closed, associative, has a two-sided identity, and gives every element a two-sided inverse. These four axioms are exactly what is needed to model symmetry — any collection of invertible transformations closed under composition (rotations of a polygon, permutations of a deck, integer addition) is a group, and conversely every group is realizable as such transformations (Cayley). The axioms matter because they are simultaneously weak enough to be ubiquitous and strong enough to force deep structure: Lagrange's theorem, the rigidity of finite simple groups, and the classification programs all flow from these four lines. Stripping arithmetic down to \"compose and undo\" is the move that lets one theorem speak about numbers, matrices, knots, and crystals at once.",
    "intuition": "Think of a group as the set of all \"moves\" you can make on an object that can always be undone, where doing two moves in a row is again a move. The identity is \"do nothing\"; the inverse is \"press undo\"; associativity says only the sequence of moves matters, not how you mentally bracket them. Geometrically, the elements ARE the symmetries: turn a square 90 degrees, flip it — each is a move, the collection of all of them is the group D_4. Abelian means order never matters (translations: walk east then north = north then east); non-abelian means it does (a Rubik's cube turn then a flip is generally not the flip then the turn). The \"order\" of an element is how many times you must repeat one move to get back to \"do nothing\", and the order of the group is just how many distinct moves exist.",
    "keyFacts": "Axioms (G, *): closure a*b in G; associativity (a*b)*c = a*(b*c); identity e with e*a = a*e = a; inverse a^{-1} with a*a^{-1} = a^{-1}*a = e.\nUniqueness: e is unique; each a^{-1} is unique. Socks-shoes: (a*b)^{-1} = b^{-1}*a^{-1}; (a^{-1})^{-1} = a.\nCancellation: a*x = a*y  =>  x = y  (and on the right). Equations a*x = b have the unique solution x = a^{-1}*b.\nAbelian: a*b = b*a for all a,b. Smallest non-abelian group is S_3, |S_3| = 6.\nOrder of group: |G| = number of elements. Order of element: |a| = least n>0 with a^n = e (infinite if none).\nFinite-group fact: |a| divides |G| (since <a> is a subgroup; Lagrange). Also a^{|G|} = e.\n|a^{-1}| = |a|; |a| = |b a b^{-1}| (conjugate elements share order).\nSmall-order facts: |G| = p prime => G cyclic, |G| = Z_p; every group of order <= 5 is abelian; order p^2 is abelian; there are exactly 5 groups of order 8 (3 abelian, 2 non-abelian: D_4, Q_8).",
    "applications": "Symmetry of physical systems: point groups and space groups classify the 32 crystal classes and 230 crystallographic space groups in crystallography and the 17 wallpaper groups for periodic tilings. In physics, the group axioms underwrite conservation laws via Noether (each continuous symmetry group gives a conserved quantity) and the gauge groups U(1), SU(2), SU(3) of the Standard Model. Modular arithmetic and the unit group (Z/n)^* drive RSA and Diffie-Hellman, where Fermat/Euler are just a^{|G|} = e for that group. Permutation groups model card shuffles, sorting-network correctness, and error-correcting codes. The Rubik's cube is literally a finitely generated group, and Polya's enumeration counts colorings up to symmetry (necklaces, chemical isomers) by group actions. Associativity-plus-inverse is also the abstract backbone of \"undo/redo\" stacks and of reversible computing.",
