    "id": "at-spaces",
    "title": "Spaces & invariants",
    "tier": "foundations",
    "symbol": "top",
    "idea": "A topological space is a set together with a chosen collection of \"open\" subsets (closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections, including the empty set and the whole space). This is the minimal scaffolding that makes continuity, limits, and connectedness meaningful without any notion of distance. Algebraic topology then asks: when are two spaces \"the same\"? It answers by attaching algebraic objects (numbers, groups, rings) that are unchanged under the allowed deformations, so that different invariants force different spaces. This converts a hard geometric question (is X homeomorphic to Y?) into a checkable algebraic one.",
    "intuition": "Picture a space made of infinitely stretchy rubber with no ruler: you may bend, stretch, and squash, but never tear or glue. Two shapes are homeomorphic if one rubber model can be reshaped into the other; the classic slogan is that a coffee mug equals a donut because each has exactly one hole. Homotopy is looser still — it lets you continuously shrink and collapse (a solid disk and a single point are homotopy equivalent), so it remembers holes but forgets dimension and thickness. An invariant is a \"fingerprint\" computed from the rubber model that comes out the same no matter how you reshape it; if two fingerprints differ, the shapes are genuinely different. The whole subject is the art of designing fingerprints sensitive enough to tell spaces apart yet computable in practice.",
    "keyFacts": "Topology axioms: emptyset,X open; arbitrary unions of opens open; finite intersections of opens open.\nContinuous f: preimage of every open set is open.\nHomeomorphism: continuous bijection f with continuous inverse (X ~= Y).\nHomotopy equiv: f:X->Y, g:Y->X with g.f ~ id_X and f.g ~ id_Y (X ~_h Y).\nHomeomorphic => homotopy equivalent; converse FALSE (disk ~_h point, not homeomorphic).\nInvariant I: X ~= Y => I(X) = I(Y); so I(X) != I(Y) => X not~= Y. (Contrapositive is the tool.)\nExamples: chi(S^2)=2, chi(T^2)=0; pi_1(S^1)=Z, pi_1(S^2)=0; b_0 = #path-components.\nInvariance of domain (Brouwer): R^m ~= R^n => m = n.",
    "applications": "Distinguishing surfaces and manifolds: the genus / Euler characteristic classifies closed orientable surfaces, and pi_1 separates the sphere from the torus. Invariance of dimension (R^m not homeomorphic to R^n for m != n) underwrites that \"dimension\" is well defined. In data analysis, persistent homology reads off the number and scale of holes in point clouds to summarize shape (TDA). In physics, topological invariants classify defects, vortices, and phases of matter (e.g. the integer quantum Hall conductance is a Chern number, a topological invariant). In robotics, configuration spaces are topological spaces whose connectivity dictates which motions are possible. In computer graphics and geometry processing, mesh genus and connected-component counts are topological checks for valid models. Compactness and connectedness, the two most basic invariants, guarantee max/min existence (extreme value theorem) and the intermediate value theorem.",
