    "symbol": "htpy",
    "idea": "A homotopy is a continuous deformation of one map into another: f, g: X -> Y are homotopic (f ~ g) if there is a continuous H: X x [0,1] -> Y with H(-,0) = f and H(-,1) = g. Two spaces are homotopy equivalent if there are maps back and forth whose composites are homotopic to the identities, so they are \"the same up to continuous bending, stretching, and collapsing\" — strictly coarser than homeomorphism. This is the central equivalence relation of algebraic topology: nearly every invariant we build (pi_n, homology, cohomology) is a homotopy invariant, meaning it cannot distinguish homotopy-equivalent spaces. Working \"up to homotopy\" is what makes the subject computable — it lets us replace a messy space by a simple model (a point, a wedge of circles, a CW complex) without losing the algebra we care about.",
    "intuition": "Picture a movie: at time t=0 you see the map f, at t=1 you see g, and every frame in between is a legal continuous map; the homotopy is the whole film. Homotopy equivalence is the relation \"same shape if you may stretch and squash but never tear or glue.\" A solid disk, a point, and all of R^n look identical to this eye (each contractible), and a coffee mug, a thick annulus, and a circle all look like \"one loop.\" Contractibility means the identity map can be filmed shrinking the whole space down to a single point. The slogan: homeomorphism remembers exact geometry; homotopy equivalence remembers only the holes.",
    "keyFacts": "f ~ g iff exists H: X x I -> Y, H_0 = f, H_1 = g; \"~\" is an equivalence relation on C(X,Y)\nf: X -> Y is a homotopy equivalence iff exists g: Y -> X with g.f ~ id_X and f.g ~ id_Y; write X ~= Y (homotopy equivalent)\nX contractible :iff id_X ~ const :iff X ~= {point}; equivalently the cone on any point map\nDeformation retract A in X (H: X x I -> X, H_0 = id, H_1(X) subset A, H_t|A = id) => inclusion A -> X is a homotopy equivalence\nHomotopy invariance: X ~= Y => pi_n(X,x) iso pi_n(Y,y) (all n), H_n(X) iso H_n(Y), H^n(X) iso H^n(Y); chi(X) is a homotopy invariant\nHomotopy category hTop: objects = spaces, morphisms = homotopy classes [X,Y] = C(X,Y)/~; composition well defined; isos = homotopy equivalences\nWhitehead: a weak homotopy equivalence (iso on all pi_n) between CW complexes IS a homotopy equivalence\nConvexity: any convex (or star-shaped) subset of R^n is contractible via straight-line homotopy H(x,t) = (1-t)x + t*x_0",
    "applications": "Inside math: it is the working notion of \"sameness\" that makes pi_1, homology, and cohomology computable, since one replaces a space by a homotopy-equivalent CW model; deformation retracts power Mayer-Vietoris and Seifert-van Kampen computations (a punctured plane retracts to S^1). Fixed-point and existence results (Brouwer's theorem is proved by a homotopy/no-retraction argument; nonexistence of a retraction D^n -> S^{n-1}). In differential geometry, the Poincare lemma says a contractible (star-shaped) region has trivial de Rham cohomology, so every closed form is exact there. In physics, homotopy classes of maps classify topological defects (vortices, monopoles, textures via pi_n), and homotopy of paths underlies gauge theory and the Aharonov-Bohm phase. In data analysis, persistent homology reports homotopy-type features (loops, voids) that survive scale changes. In robotics/motion planning, configuration paths are planned up to homotopy to capture genuinely distinct routes around obstacles.",
