    "symbol": "dR",
    "idea": "De Rham cohomology builds a cohomology theory on a smooth manifold purely out of calculus: the chain groups are spaces of differential k-forms and the coboundary is the exterior derivative d. Because d∘d = 0, \"closed modulo exact\" forms (ker d / im d) form graded vector spaces H^k_dR(M;R) that measure the failure of closed forms to be exact -- exactly the obstruction you meet when a field with zero curl is not a gradient. The de Rham theorem then says these analytically-defined groups agree with the purely topological singular cohomology H^k(M;R), so an integration computation detects holes. This is the cleanest bridge between analysis on manifolds and algebraic topology, and it makes invariants like Betti numbers computable by solving differential equations.",
    "intuition": "Picture a differential k-form as a gadget you can integrate over a k-dimensional piece of the manifold; d turns a k-form into the (k+1)-form whose integral over a region equals the original's integral over the boundary (Stokes). \"Closed\" (dω = 0) means locally a potential exists; \"exact\" (ω = dη) means a global potential exists. The gap between them is a hole: on the punctured plane the angle form dθ is closed but not exact because looping around the puncture accumulates 2π, and that single nonzero \"winding integral\" is the 1-dimensional class in H^1. So a de Rham class is the answer to \"which cycles can this closed form detect?\", and pairing forms against cycles by integration is precisely the isomorphism with cohomology.",
