    "id": "dg-hodge",
    "title": "Hodge theory",
    "tier": "global",
    "symbol": "Hodge",
    "idea": "Hodge theory uses a Riemannian metric to single out a canonical representative inside each de Rham cohomology class. On an oriented Riemannian manifold the metric supplies a pointwise inner product on forms and a volume form; together these give the Hodge star *, a formal adjoint δ for the exterior derivative d, and the Hodge Laplacian Δ = dδ + δd. The central result (Hodge theorem) is that on a compact manifold without boundary, every de Rham class contains exactly one harmonic form (Δω = 0), so the finite-dimensional space of harmonic k-forms is naturally isomorphic to H^k_dR(M). This converts a topological invariant into the kernel of an elliptic operator, letting analysis (spectral theory, PDE estimates, curvature) speak directly about topology.",
    "intuition": "Think of the space of k-forms as an infinite-dimensional inner-product space on which d pushes \"up\" a degree and δ pulls \"down\". A form is closed if it has no d-component pointing further up; it is co-closed if it has no δ-component pointing down. A harmonic form is one that is simultaneously closed and co-closed — it sits in the \"still\" subspace that neither d nor δ moves. Each cohomology class is an affine subspace (a closed form plus all exact forms); the harmonic representative is its unique point of smallest L^2 norm, the foot of the perpendicular from the origin. The Laplacian Δ measures total restlessness ||dω||^2 + ||δω||^2, so harmonic = perfectly relaxed = energy-minimizing. The Hodge star is the geometric duality that turns a k-dimensional \"infinitesimal slab\" into its orthogonal (n-k)-dimensional complement, which is why it interchanges H^k and H^{n-k}.",
    "keyFacts": "Setup: (M^n, g) oriented Riemannian, with pointwise inner product <.,.> on Λ^k T*M and volume form vol.\nHodge star: *: Ω^k -> Ω^{n-k}, defined by  α ∧ *β = <α,β> vol  for all α,β in Ω^k.\nOn Ω^k (Riemannian signature):  ** = (-1)^{k(n-k)} id.  Also *1 = vol, *vol = 1.\nCodifferential:  δ = (-1)^{n(k+1)+1} * d *  on Ω^k. It lowers degree: δ: Ω^k -> Ω^{k-1}, and δ^2 = 0.\nAdjointness (M compact, no boundary): with (α,β) = ∫_M <α,β> vol,  (dα, β) = (α, δβ). So δ = d* is the formal L^2-adjoint.\nHodge Laplacian:  Δ = dδ + δd. It is self-adjoint, non-negative, and elliptic (2nd order, scalar principal symbol |ξ|^2).\nHarmonic forms H^k = ker(Δ|_{Ω^k}). On compact M:  Δω = 0  ⟺  dω = 0 and δω = 0  (since (Δω,ω)=||dω||^2+||δω||^2).\nHodge decomposition (compact, no boundary), L^2-orthogonal:  Ω^k = H^k ⊕ d(Ω^{k-1}) ⊕ δ(Ω^{k+1}).  Closed forms = H^k ⊕ d(Ω^{k-1}).\nHodge theorem:  H^k ≅ H^k_dR(M); each class has a unique harmonic representative. dim H^k = b_k(M) < ∞.\nPoincaré duality: * commutes with Δ (up to the sign convention) and maps H^k -> H^{n-k} isomorphically, so b_k = b_{n-k}.\nWeitzenböck/Bochner (1-forms): Δ = ∇*∇ + Ric. Hence compact M with Ric > 0 ⟹ b_1 = 0.",
    "applications": "Inside mathematics: proves finiteness of Betti numbers and Poincaré duality for compact oriented manifolds analytically; underlies Hodge theory of compact Kähler and projective manifolds (the Hodge decomposition H^k = ⊕ H^{p,q}, hard Lefschetz, and the Hodge conjecture); Bochner technique forces vanishing of cohomology from positive curvature (Ric>0 kills b_1; Bochner–Kodaira/Kodaira vanishing in complex geometry). It is the prototype for the Atiyah–Singer index theorem, where the Euler characteristic and signature appear as indices of the de Rham and signature operators. In number theory and arithmetic geometry, Hodge structures and the comparison of de Rham, Betti, and ℓ-adic cohomology organize periods and motives. Outside pure mathematics: in physics, harmonic forms are zero modes — the Hodge decomposition is the covariant Helmholtz decomposition behind Maxwell theory (potentials, gauge fixing, magnetic monopoles via H^2), Yang–Mills, and the counting of moduli/massless fields in string compactifications on Calabi–Yau manifolds. In computational geometry and graphics, discrete exterior calculus and discrete Hodge decomposition split vector fields into curl-free, divergence-free, and harmonic parts for fluid simulation, mesh parameterization, and topology-aware vector-field design.",
