    "symbol": "LDP",
    "idea": "Large deviation theory quantifies the exponentially small probability of rare events — outcomes far from the law-of-large-numbers limit. Where the CLT describes fluctuations on the sqrt(n) scale, large deviations describe atypical excursions on the scale of n itself: P(empirical mean ~ x) decays like exp(-n*I(x)), and the *rate function* I packages the exponential cost of each deviation. This matters because rare events drive risk (insurance ruin, queue overflow, error probabilities in coding), because the rate function reveals the *most likely way* a rare event happens, and because the same machinery (Legendre transforms, tilting, sub-Gaussian tails) underlies modern concentration-of-measure, the workhorse of high-dimensional statistics and learning theory.",
    "intuition": "Picture the distribution of the sample mean S_n/n piling up at the true mean mu (LLN). The bulk wiggle is Gaussian (CLT), but the *tails* are governed by a deterministic landscape: -1/n * log P(S_n/n ~ x) -> I(x), a convex valley with its zero at x=mu and steep walls elsewhere. The rate function is the Legendre transform of the log-MGF (cumulant generating function), so convex duality is the engine: tilting the measure by e^{theta*x} shifts the mean to x, and the exponential price of that tilt is exactly I(x). For Sanov, the 'point' x becomes an entire empirical distribution and I becomes relative entropy (KL divergence) — distance is measured by how hard it is to fake a given law from i.i.d. samples. Concentration is the same picture read as a guarantee: if every coordinate moves the function only a little (bounded differences) the function clusters in an exponentially thin shell around its mean.",
    "keyFacts": "Log-MGF / cumulant gen. fn: Lambda(theta)=log E[e^{theta X_1}]; assume 0 in interior of {Lambda<inf}.\nLegendre-Fenchel rate fn: I(x)=sup_theta (theta*x - Lambda(theta)); I is convex, lsc, I(mu)=0.\nCramer (R, i.i.d.): for closed F, limsup (1/n) log P(S_n/n in F) <= -inf_F I; for open G, liminf (1/n) log P(S_n/n in G) >= -inf_G I. Upper bound for closed F holds always (Chernoff); full LDP needs the steepness/finiteness above.\nChernoff bound: P(S_n/n >= a) <= exp(-n*I(a)) for a>mu (just optimize Markov on e^{theta S_n}).\nGartner-Ellis: replaces i.i.d. by existence of Lambda(theta)=lim (1/n) log E[e^{theta S_n}], essentially smooth + lsc -> LDP with rate Lambda*.\nSanov (empirical measure L_n on alphabet/Polish space): LDP in weak topology with rate I(nu)=H(nu||mu)=KL(nu||mu)=int log(dnu/dmu) dnu (=+inf if nu not<<mu).\nContraction principle: if X_n satisfies LDP with rate I and f continuous, f(X_n) satisfies LDP with rate J(y)=inf{I(x): f(x)=y}. (Cramer = Sanov via the mean map, by contraction.)\nVaradhan's lemma: under LDP + tail condition, (1/n) log E[e^{n F(X_n)}] -> sup_x (F(x)-I(x)).\nGaussian rate (X~N(0,sigma^2)): I(x)=x^2/(2 sigma^2).\nHoeffding: X_i in [a_i,b_i] indep, mean-zero sums: P(S_n>=t) <= exp(-2 t^2 / sum (b_i-a_i)^2).\nBernstein: with var bound v and a.s. bound M, P(S_n>=t) <= exp(-t^2 / (2(v + M t/3))) (sub-exponential crossover).\nMcDiarmid (bounded differences): f with |f(...,x_k,...)-f(...,x_k',...)|<=c_k => P(f-E f >= t) <= exp(-2 t^2 / sum c_k^2).\nSub-Gaussian: E[e^{theta(X-EX)}] <= e^{sigma^2 theta^2/2} for all theta => two-sided tail <= 2 exp(-t^2/(2 sigma^2)).",
    "applications": "Statistics: error exponents of hypothesis tests — Stein's lemma and Chernoff information give the optimal exponential decay of type-I/II errors via KL divergence. Information theory: Sanov underlies the method of types, channel/source coding error exponents, and the operational meaning of relative entropy. Machine learning / high-dim statistics: concentration (Hoeffding, Bernstein, McDiarmid, bounded-differences, sub-Gaussian/sub-exponential tails) gives generalization bounds, empirical-process control, and matrix-concentration for randomized linear algebra. Queueing & networks: large-deviation buffer-overflow / effective-bandwidth estimates size telecom and packet buffers. Insurance & finance: Cramer-Lundberg ruin theory and exponential tail estimates for portfolio loss / VaR. Statistical physics: the rate function IS the free energy / entropy (Gibbs variational principle); Cramer is the thermodynamic equivalence of ensembles. Rare-event simulation: importance sampling tilts by the LDP-optimal change of measure to make rare events frequent. Random graphs & combinatorics: upper tail of triangle counts, concentration of chromatic number via McDiarmid.",
