    "id": "opt-convex-functions",
    "title": "Convex functions & subgradients",
    "tier": "foundations",
    "symbol": "cvx f",
    "idea": "A function f : R^n -> R u {+inf} is convex when its epigraph {(x,t) : f(x) <= t} is a convex set — equivalently, when every chord lies on or above the graph. That one hypothesis buys the two things optimization most needs: every local minimum is a global minimum, and the first-order condition 0 in ∂f(x*) is not merely necessary but sufficient, i.e. an optimality certificate you can check. Convexity also comes with a calculus — nonnegative sums, pointwise suprema, affine precomposition, perspective, partial minimization and infimal convolution all preserve it — so convexity is usually recognized structurally, from how a model was built, rather than by testing Hessians. Its two quantitative refinements, strong convexity (curvature at least μ) and L-smoothness (curvature at most L), turn the qualitative statement into explicit convergence rates governed by the condition number κ = L/μ, while subgradients extend the whole theory to the nondifferentiable objects — |x|, ‖x‖_1, max, indicator functions — that regularized modern problems are actually made of.",
    "intuition": "Picture a bowl: pick two points on the graph, stretch a string between them, and the string never dips below the surface. Flip the picture upward and the same statement is that the region above the graph (the epigraph) is a convex set, which is why every fact about convex sets — separation, supporting hyperplanes, normal cones — immediately becomes a fact about convex functions. The single most important consequence is that a tangent plane is a *global* underestimator: f(y) >= f(x) + <grad f(x), y - x> everywhere, so purely local slope information tells you something true about the whole space. That is the mechanism behind \"local min = global min\", and it is why a convex function is exactly the pointwise supremum of the affine functions lying under it — the graph is the upper envelope of its own tangents. Where the graph has a kink there is no unique tangent but a whole fan of supporting lines; the set of their slopes is the subdifferential ∂f(x), and flatness at the bottom of the bowl means the fan contains 0. Finally, strong convexity and smoothness say the graph is sandwiched between two paraboloids of curvature μ and L: the level sets are ellipsoids of eccentricity at most κ = L/μ, and gradient methods crawl along the long axis of that ellipse exactly κ times slower than along the short one.",
    "keyFacts": "f : R^n -> R u {+inf} convex  <=>  epi f = {(x,t) : f(x) <= t} convex  <=>  f(θx+(1-θ)y) <= θf(x)+(1-θ)f(y) for all x,y in dom f, θ in [0,1]\nproper: dom f != ∅ and f > -inf everywhere.  closed: epi f closed  <=>  f lower semicontinuous\nJensen: f convex, X an integrable random vector with X in dom f a.s.  =>  f(E X) <= E f(X)  (RHS may be +inf); if f is strictly convex, equality iff X is a.s. constant\n1st order (f differentiable on an OPEN convex D): f convex on D  <=>  f(y) >= f(x) + <grad f(x), y-x> for all x,y in D  <=>  <grad f(x)-grad f(y), x-y> >= 0 (monotone gradient)\n2nd order (f twice differentiable on an OPEN convex D): f convex  <=>  Hess f(x) PSD for all x in D.  Hess f > 0 => strictly convex; the converse is FALSE (x^4 at 0)\nrestriction to lines: f convex  <=>  t |-> f(x + t v) is convex on its domain for every x, v\npreserved by: nonnegative combinations; pointwise sup over an arbitrary index set; x |-> f(Ax+b); perspective (x,t) |-> t f(x/t) on t>0; partial minimization of a jointly convex F; inf-convolution; h(f_1,...,f_k) with h convex and nondecreasing in each argument\nsubgradient: g in ∂f(x)  <=>  f(y) >= f(x) + <g, y-x> for ALL y.  ∂f(x) is closed and convex; for f proper convex it is nonempty for x in ri(dom f), and nonempty AND compact for x in int(dom f)\nf proper convex, x in int dom f: f is differentiable at x  <=>  ∂f(x) is a singleton, and then ∂f(x) = {grad f(x)}\nFermat's rule: x* is a GLOBAL minimizer of f  <=>  0 in ∂f(x*)  (immediate from the definition; no differentiability, no constraint qualification)\nsum rule: ∂(f+g)(x) ⊇ ∂f(x)+∂g(x) always; equality if ri dom f ∩ ri dom g != ∅ (Moreau–Rockafellar).  chain: ∂(f∘A)(x) ⊇ A^T ∂f(Ax), equality if ri dom f ∩ range(A) != ∅\nmax rule: f = max_i f_i (finitely many convex, differentiable)  =>  ∂f(x) = conv{grad f_i(x) : f_i(x) = f(x)}\nμ-strong convexity in ‖·‖_2: f - (μ/2)‖x‖^2 convex  <=>  f(y) >= f(x)+<g,y-x>+(μ/2)‖y-x‖^2 for all g in ∂f(x)  <=>  <g_x-g_y,x-y> >= μ‖x-y‖^2  <=>  Hess f >= μI (C^2 case)\nf proper closed μ-strongly convex => the minimizer x* exists and is unique; quadratic growth f(x)-f* >= (μ/2)‖x-x*‖^2; and (f differentiable) Polyak–Lojasiewicz ‖grad f(x)‖^2 >= 2μ(f(x)-f*)\nL-smooth (f differentiable, grad f L-Lipschitz — convexity NOT required): descent lemma |f(y) - f(x) - <grad f(x),y-x>| <= (L/2)‖y-x‖^2\nf convex AND differentiable: grad f is L-Lipschitz  <=>  <grad f(x)-grad f(y),x-y> >= (1/L)‖grad f(x)-grad f(y)‖^2 (Baillon–Haddad cocoercivity)  <=>  f(y) >= f(x)+<grad f(x),y-x>+(1/2L)‖grad f(y)-grad f(x)‖^2\nrates: gradient descent with step 1/L gives f(x_k)-f* <= L‖x_0-x*‖^2/(2k) if f is convex L-smooth, and ‖x_k-x*‖^2 <= (1-μ/L)^k ‖x_0-x*‖^2 if also μ-strongly convex; κ = L/μ\nregularity: f convex and finite on an open U ⊆ R^n => f is locally Lipschitz on U, differentiable off a Lebesgue-null set, and twice differentiable a.e. in Alexandrov's sense (a.e., NOT everywhere)\nf proper closed convex  =>  f is the pointwise sup of its affine minorants, i.e. f** = f (Fenchel–Moreau)",
    "applications": "Machine learning and statistics: logistic regression, SVMs (hinge loss), LASSO and group-lasso, matrix completion with the nuclear norm — all convex, most of them nonsmooth, so subgradients and proximal steps are the working tools; maximum likelihood for an exponential family is concave because the log-partition function is convex; EM and variational inference (the ELBO) are literally Jensen's inequality applied to a log; KL(p‖q) >= 0 and the whole information inequality are Jensen. Probability and large deviations: the cumulant generating function log E e^{λX} is convex (Hölder), which is what makes Chernoff bounds work, and Cramér's rate function is its Legendre transform. Economics and decision theory: concave utility IS risk aversion, since Jensen gives E u(X) <= u(E X); cost and profit functions are convex/concave by construction via partial minimization. Statistical physics: thermodynamic potentials are convex/concave in their natural variables and related by Legendre transforms; stability of matter is a convexity statement. Control and engineering: Lyapunov conditions and LMIs, SDP relaxations, model predictive control, filter design. Signal and image processing: total-variation denoising, compressed sensing, sparse deconvolution. Geometry and analysis: support functions of convex bodies, Brunn–Minkowski and log-concavity (Prékopa–Leindler), Alexandrov's theorem, and classical inequality-proving — AM-GM, Hölder, Minkowski, Hadamard's three-lines theorem (log-convexity). Inside optimization itself, L and μ are the numbers that predict iteration counts, and self-concordance (a third-derivative convexity condition) is what makes interior-point methods polynomial.",
