      {
        "name": "Domains: Euclidean, PID, UFD, and where factorization breaks",
        "gist": "Among domains there is a strict hierarchy fields ⊂ Euclidean ⊂ PID ⊂ UFD ⊂ domains, and the failure of unique factorization of elements is exactly what ideal-theoretic factorization was invented to repair. Knowing the strictness examples prevents most false conjectures.",
        "examples": [
          "Z, k[x], Z[i] are Euclidean; Z[(1 + sqrt(-19))/2] is a PID that is not Euclidean",
          "k[x,y] is a UFD (Gauss: R UFD => R[x] UFD) but not a PID, since (x,y) is not principal",
          "Z[sqrt(-5)]: 6 = 2*3 = (1+sqrt(-5))(1-sqrt(-5)) fails unique factorization of elements, yet ideals factor uniquely (Dedekind)",
          "In a PID every nonzero prime ideal is maximal; k[x,y] shows this fails in dimension 2",
          "Local rings: Z_(p), k[[x]], germs of smooth functions — one maximal ideal, so 'one point with fuzz'"
        ]
      },
      {
        "name": "Homomorphisms, extension and contraction, and the first geometric dictionary",
        "gist": "A ring map f : R -> S pulls ideals back (contraction, q |-> f^{-1}(q)) and pushes them forward (extension, I |-> IS), and only contraction respects primality unconditionally. This asymmetry is the algebraic source of the contravariance of Spec and of everything about morphisms later.",
        "examples": [
          "Contraction of a prime is prime; contraction of a maximal need not be maximal (Z -> Q, m = (0))",
          "Extension of a prime need not be prime: 5Z[i] = (2+i)(2-i); 2Z[i] = (1+i)^2 ramifies",
          "If S is a finitely generated k-algebra and R -> S a k-algebra map, contractions of maximal ideals are maximal (via Zariski's lemma)",
          "I(V) and V(I) for a set of polynomials: the order-reversing pair that the Nullstellensatz will make a bijection on radical ideals",
          "Quotient maps R -> R/I and their duals: Spec(R/I) is the closed subset V(I) of Spec(R)"
        ]
      }
