          "V(I ∩ J) = V(I) ∪ V(J) and V(sum_a I_a) = ∩_a V(I_a): finite unions and arbitrary intersections stay algebraic",
          "I(V((x^2))) = (x): V forgets multiplicities, which is exactly why only radical ideals can be recovered",
          "for any subset X of A^n, V(I(X)) is the Zariski closure of X — e.g. the closure of Z^2 inside A^2_C is all of A^2",
          "over an infinite field the Zariski-closed subsets of A^1 are precisely the finite sets together with A^1 itself"
