        "name": "Topological conjugacy and equivalence",
        "gist": "Conjugacy is a homeomorphism intertwining two flows time-for-time; equivalence only asks that orbits map to orbits preserving the direction of time, permitting a reparametrization of the clock. Topological rather than smooth conjugacy is the right relation because linearization theorems can only deliver a homeomorphism.",
        "examples": [
          "hyperbolic linear flows on R^n are conjugate exactly when their stable subspaces have the same dimension: x' = -x and x' = -2x are conjugate via h(x) = x|x|, though no C^1 conjugacy exists since a smooth conjugacy would have to preserve the eigenvalues -1 and -2",
          "Hartman-Grobman gives local topological conjugacy to the linearization at a hyperbolic equilibrium; Sternberg's theorem upgrades this to C^k conjugacy under non-resonance conditions on the eigenvalues",
          "time rescaling: phi and (t,x) |-> phi(ct,x) with c > 0 are topologically equivalent but generally not conjugate — all periods and the entropy get scaled",
          "the logistic map f(x) = 4x(1-x) and the tent map are conjugate by h(x) = sin^2(pi x/2), so every dynamical fact about one transfers verbatim to the other",
          "structural stability (conjugacy class stable under perturbation): Peixoto's theorem characterizes and proves genericity of structurally stable C^1 fields on compact orientable surfaces; in dimension >= 3 they are not dense (Smale), the C^1 stability conjecture is a theorem (Mane for diffeomorphisms, Hayashi for flows), and the C^r case for r >= 2 is still open"
        ]
