    "idea": "Divisibility theory studies the multiplicative skeleton of Z: which integers divide which, how to compute greatest common divisors fast, and the fact that every integer above 1 factors into primes in essentially one way. One engine drives all of it — the division algorithm — which yields the Euclidean algorithm, then Bezout's identity, then Euclid's lemma, then the fundamental theorem of arithmetic (FTA). It matters because unique factorization is the license to argue \"one prime at a time\": multiplicativity of arithmetic functions, Euler products, valuations, and most congruence manipulations all silently invoke it. It matters just as much where it fails: unique factorization of *elements* breaks down in general rings of algebraic integers, and repairing it with ideals is exactly what launches algebraic number theory.",
    "intuition": "Think of the positive integers under multiplication as a free commutative monoid on the primes: an integer is a vector of exponents (v_2, v_3, v_5, ...) with finitely many nonzero entries. Multiplication adds vectors, divisibility is componentwise <=, gcd is componentwise min, lcm is componentwise max. So (N, |) is a lattice whose atoms are the primes, and FTA is precisely the claim that the coordinate map n -> (v_p(n))_p is a bijection onto finitely-supported nonnegative vectors. The Euclidean algorithm is the ancient picture of measuring one stick against another (anthyphairesis), or greedily tiling an a-by-b rectangle with squares: the leftover shrinks geometrically, so the process halts in O(log) steps and its quotient sequence is literally the continued-fraction expansion of a/b. Bezout says the set {ax + by} is not a scattered cloud but an arithmetic progression through 0 — the subgroup generated by its smallest positive member, which is gcd(a,b). Geometrically, gcd(a,b) counts visibility: exactly gcd(a,b) - 1 lattice points lie strictly inside the segment from (0,0) to (a,b), so (a,b) is visible from the origin iff a and b are coprime.",
    "keyFacts": "Division algorithm: for a,b in Z with b != 0 there exist UNIQUE q,r in Z with a = qb + r and 0 <= r < |b|.\nGCD as an ideal: for a,b not both 0, aZ + bZ = dZ with d = gcd(a,b) > 0. Hence Bezout: there exist x,y in Z with ax + by = gcd(a,b).\nEuclid step: for b != 0, gcd(a,b) = gcd(b, a mod b); remainders strictly decrease, so it terminates. Lame: the number of divisions is <= 5 * (number of decimal digits of min(|a|,|b|)); the worst case is consecutive Fibonacci numbers.\nEuclid's lemma: if p is PRIME and p | ab then p | a or p | b. Primality is essential: 6 | 4*3 but 6 does not divide 4 or 3. General form: if gcd(a,n) = 1 and n | ab then n | b.\nFTA: every integer n >= 1 is a product of primes (empty product for n = 1), unique up to order; equivalently n = prod_p p^{v_p(n)}. Existence needs only well-ordering/strong induction; UNIQUENESS needs Euclid's lemma.\nValuations: v_p(ab) = v_p(a) + v_p(b); v_p(a+b) >= min(v_p(a), v_p(b)) with equality when v_p(a) != v_p(b); and a | b (b != 0) iff v_p(a) <= v_p(b) for all p.\ngcd/lcm: v_p(gcd(a,b)) = min, v_p(lcm(a,b)) = max; for a,b != 0, gcd(a,b) * lcm(a,b) = |ab| (no analogue for three or more arguments).\nLegendre: v_p(n!) = sum_{i>=1} floor(n/p^i) = (n - s_p(n))/(p-1), s_p = digit sum base p. Kummer: v_p(C(m+n,m)) = number of carries when adding m and n in base p.\nLinear Diophantine: ax + by = c is solvable in Z iff g = gcd(a,b) divides c; the full solution set is (x0 + (b/g)t, y0 - (a/g)t), t in Z.\nEuclid's theorem: for any finite set S of primes, 1 + prod(S) has a prime factor outside S, so there are infinitely many primes. That number need NOT be prime: 2*3*5*7*11*13 + 1 = 30031 = 59 * 509.\nEuler/Mertens (strictly stronger than Euclid): sum_{p <= x} 1/p = log log x + M + O(1/log x), so sum_p 1/p diverges.\nBertrand-Chebyshev: for every integer n >= 1 there is a prime p with n < p <= 2n (a theorem, not a postulate, since Chebyshev 1852).\nStructure: Z is Euclidean (with |.|) => PID => UFD; both implications are strict — Z[x] is a UFD that is not a PID, and Z[(1+sqrt(-19))/2] is a PID admitting NO Euclidean function.\nIn any integral domain prime => irreducible; the converse holds in UFDs but fails in Z[sqrt(-5)], where 6 = 2*3 = (1+sqrt(-5))(1-sqrt(-5)) with all four factors irreducible and pairwise non-associate.",
    "applications": "Inside mathematics: modular arithmetic and the Chinese remainder theorem rest on Bezout; multiplicativity of arithmetic functions and the Euler product for zeta are FTA restated analytically; p-adic valuations grow into p-adic absolute values, Ostrowski's classification and local fields; Gauss's lemma exports factorization from Z to Q[x] and underwrites Eisenstein's criterion and Galois-theoretic irreducibility arguments; the structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a PID (via Smith normal form) classifies finite abelian groups and produces rational and Jordan canonical forms; polynomial gcd, resultants and subresultants power symbolic integration, elimination theory and computer algebra; the Euclidean algorithm's quotient sequence IS the continued fraction expansion, feeding Diophantine approximation and Pell's equation. Outside pure mathematics: RSA key generation computes the private exponent as a modular inverse by extended Euclid, and the whole public-key edifice rests on the asymmetry that gcd is quasi-linear while no polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm is known (that hardness is unproven, and Shor's algorithm factors in quantum polynomial time); Reed-Solomon and BCH decoders solve the key equation with a truncated extended Euclid; residue number systems and CRT-based arithmetic speed up big-integer computation; rational reconstruction recovers exact fractions from modular data; lcm gives hyperperiods in real-time task scheduling and cycle lengths in gear trains; coprime strides make linear-probing hash tables and linear congruential generators traverse full cycles; sample-rate conversion by p/q in lowest terms, moire and aliasing patterns, and Bresenham-style line rasterization are gcd phenomena; Euclidean rhythms (Bjorklund's algorithm, popularized by Toussaint) distribute k onsets among n beats by running the Euclidean algorithm.",
