        "name": "Computing units and the regulator",
        "gist": "Fundamental units can be astronomically large, so computing them is a genuine algorithmic problem: subexponential classically under GRH, and one of the few natural problems with a quantum speedup that is not a hidden-subgroup-of-a-finite-group problem.",
        "examples": [
          "Continued fractions / infrastructure (Shanks) for real quadratic fields; log eps can be of size ~ sqrt(d) log d, so eps has exponentially many digits and needs a compact representation",
          "Buchmann's algorithm: class group and unit group together, subexponential L(1/2) under GRH; bnfinit in Pari/GP, and Magma's implementations (outputs are GRH-conditional unless certified)",
          "Hallgren (2002): quantum polynomial-time algorithm for Pell / the regulator in fixed degree; Eisentraeger-Hallgren-Kitaev-Song (2014) for unit groups beyond fixed degree",
          "Cramer-Ducas-Peikert-Regev: recovering short generators of principal ideals in cyclotomic rings by decoding in the log-unit lattice, breaking Soliloquy / Smart-Vercauteren style schemes when combined with Biasse-Song",
          "Verifying a claimed fundamental unit is easy; proving minimality unconditionally is the expensive part (Minkowski bounds, or GRH-conditional certificates)"
        ]
