          "Wirsing's theorem: for real non-negative multiplicative f with suitable growth conditions and sum_{p<=x} f(p) ~ tau x/log x, the mean value exists and is given by an explicit Euler-product constant",
          "Halasz's theorem: a multiplicative f with |f| <= 1 has mean value tending to 0 unless f 'pretends to be' n^{it} for some real t, quantified by a distance on primes",
          "Delange's theorem: for |f| <= 1 multiplicative with sum_p (1-f(p))/p convergent, the mean value equals prod_p (1-1/p) sum_j f(p^j)p^{-j}",
          "Selberg-Delange method: asymptotics for sum_{n<=x} f(n) when the Dirichlet series is zeta(s)^z times something regular, e.g. counting integers with exactly k prime factors (Landau: pi_k(x) ~ x (log log x)^{k-1}/((k-1)! log x))",
          "Granville-Soundararajan 'pretentious' number theory: recasting PNT-type results as statements that mu does not pretend to be any n^{it}"
